      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The U.S. Constitution vests the executive power of the United States in the President (Art. II, §1).",
      "evidence": "Text of the Constitution; legal commentary confirming that executive authority, including the power to issue directives, resides with the President.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Federal law defines an executive order as a directive issued by the President or by persons acting under authority expressly delegated by the President.",
      "evidence": "5 U.S.C. § 301 and related statutes; Supreme Court cases (e.g., *Youngstown Sheet & Tube Co. v. Sawyer*) interpreting the scope of presidential directives.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Delegated authority to issue such directives can be given only to individuals who hold a position within the U.S. executive branch, which under 5 U.S.C. § 3326 requires U.S. citizenship.",
      "evidence": "Statutory requirement that federal officers and employees be U.S. citizens; agency regulations confirming citizenship as a prerequisite for appointment.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "John Key holds no U.S. executive‑branch position and is not a U.S. citizen.",
      "evidence": "Public records show no appointment of John Key to any U.S. federal office; his citizenship status remains New Zealand only.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Because John Key lacks both the constitutional and statutory authority to issue an executive order, he cannot issue one in the United States.",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 1‑5: authority to issue executive orders is limited to the President or delegated U.S. officials, which John Key is not.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
    }
  ],
